  "query_preview": "/FRIDAY THROUGH WEDNESDAY/...\nIssued at 318 PM CDT Wed Aug 19 2026\n\nThe latest NAM12 and GFS solns develop a warm front from \nnorthwestern into south central Nebraska Friday. This is in \nresponse to increasing mid level hts from the southwest. The \nlatest MET and MAV guidance has highs well into the mid and \nupper 90s across southwestern into portions of the eastern \nPanhandle Friday afternoon. Further east and along and north of \nthe warm front, lower to middle 70 dew points will push into the\nthe central and eastern Sandhills, as well as portions of \nnortheastern and central Nebraska. The latest NAM soln develops \n5000+ J/KG capes north of the front Friday afternoon. Very steep\nlow and mid level lapse rates, co-located with the high CAPE \nand deep layer shear of 50+ KTS will lead to a decent threat for\nvery large hail. Also, along and north of the warm front, low \nlevel winds will back to the east and forecast 200+ 0-2 KM \nhelicities would be indicative of a tornado threat. Forecast \nhodographs Friday afternoon north of the front also are \nindicative of a tornado threat with low level curvature noted \nfrom the surface up to H850. In the wake of exiting convection \nFriday night, a northern stream trough will force a back door \ncold front into north central and eastern Nebraska Saturday. \nThis feature will cool temperatures back to around 80 in the \nnortheastern forecast area. Further southwest, highs will reach \ninto the lower 90s over SW Nebraska. Late in the day Saturday, \nconvective development will be possible over the higher terrain \nof eastern Colorado and eastern Wyoming INVOF a theta e ridge. \nThis activity will track east Saturday evening. An active \npattern will continue early next week as a strong upper level \ntrough traverses southern Canada. This feature will suppress \nridging to the south and southwest early next week. This will \nlead to below normal temps in the northeastern forecast area \nwith continued warm readings in the SW CWA. Northwesterly and \nwesterly flow aloft will allow the pattern to remain active with\nperiodic chances for thunderstorms through the middle of next \nweek.",
